1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,845
Both statements are true. A colleague on
Windows opened the file, and the editor

2
00:00:08,885 --> 00:00:13,393
wrote it back with CRLF line endings. CRLF
and LF are two ways to mark the end of a

3
00:00:13,433 --> 00:00:16,737
line. Now every line differs
from what the repository stores.

4
00:00:16,777 --> 00:00:21,340
Review is impossible, blame for that file
is ruined, and the next merge of any other

5
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:24,464
branch that touched the file
will conflict on every line.

6
00:00:24,804 --> 00:00:31,776
The team's first fix is a chat message:
"everyone, please set core.autocrlf".

7
00:00:31,816 --> 00:00:39,061
Three weeks later it happens again, with
a contractor who never saw the message.

8
00:00:39,101 --> 00:00:46,618
Nobody did anything wrong. A configuration
value covers what one person adds on one

9
00:00:46,658 --> 00:00:52,355
machine. The team needed something
that is part of the project.

10
00:00:54,195 --> 00:00:57,204
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:57,244 --> 00:01:01,252
You met line endings as a symptom in the
working-tree part of the course, and you

12
00:01:01,292 --> 00:01:05,300
wrote ignore rules in .gitignore. Today's
file looks like a sibling of .gitignore

13
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:09,248
and is something else: .gitattributes
doesn't decide whether Git tracks a path.

14
00:01:09,288 --> 00:01:13,345
It decides how Git treats the content of
a path when it converts, compares, merges

15
00:01:13,385 --> 00:01:14,095
or archives it.

16
00:01:14,435 --> 00:01:18,289
This video covers the mechanism and
its first application, line endings.

17
00:01:18,329 --> 00:01:22,670
The next one covers the three kinds of
driver, programs that attributes can name.

18
00:01:22,710 --> 00:01:26,780
One sentence connects both, and you
should be able to say it by the end: the

19
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:28,835
attribute travels, the driver doesn't.

20
00:01:30,675 --> 00:01:39,235
say which attribute applies to a
path and from which file it comes;

21
00:01:39,575 --> 00:01:42,311
explain "the attribute
travels, the driver does not";

22
00:01:42,651 --> 00:01:47,427
normalize line endings in a repository with
text and eol, and predict what the next

23
00:01:47,467 --> 00:01:48,413
git status shows;

24
00:01:48,753 --> 00:01:51,366
renormalize existing files in one commit;

25
00:01:51,707 --> 00:01:54,816
explain what core.autocrlf
cannot do that attributes do.

26
00:01:56,656 --> 00:02:02,633
In one sentence: a .gitattributes file
attaches named attributes to paths, and Git

27
00:02:02,673 --> 00:02:07,916
consults them whenever it converts,
compares, merges or archives a file.

28
00:02:07,956 --> 00:02:13,419
The file is versioned and reaches every
clone, while the programs that some

29
00:02:13,459 --> 00:02:17,968
attributes name are defined in
configuration and reach nobody.

30
00:02:18,308 --> 00:02:21,766
Precisely. Each line is a
pattern followed by attributes.

31
00:02:21,806 --> 00:02:24,956
For one path, an attribute
is in one of four states.

32
00:02:32,096 --> 00:02:36,204
Patterns follow the rules of .gitignore
with two exceptions: negative patterns are

33
00:02:36,244 --> 00:02:40,402
forbidden, and a pattern that matches a
directory doesn't apply to the files inside

34
00:02:40,442 --> 00:02:44,095
it. Within one file a later line
overrides an earlier one, per attribute.

35
00:02:44,435 --> 00:02:49,849
Between files, the order of precedence
is: first .git/info/attributes, which is

36
00:02:49,889 --> 00:02:55,509
local to this clone and not versioned. Then
the .gitattributes in the directory of

37
00:02:55,549 --> 00:03:00,479
the path, then those of its parent
directories, the nearer the stronger.

38
00:03:00,519 --> 00:03:05,311
Then the file named by core.attributesFile.
Then the system-wide file.

39
00:03:05,651 --> 00:03:10,997
A macro attribute sets several others at
once. The one built-in macro is binary,

40
00:03:11,037 --> 00:03:13,016
defined as -diff -merge -text.

41
00:03:13,356 --> 00:03:22,801
Inside the dot git folder: nothing,
unless you use .git/info/attributes.

42
00:03:22,841 --> 00:03:29,387
A .gitattributes file is
an ordinary tracked file.

43
00:03:29,727 --> 00:03:34,577
Which attributes need configuration? text,
eol, export-ignore, -diff, -merge, the

44
00:03:34,617 --> 00:03:39,648
built-in diff patterns and the built-in
merge drivers work from the attribute alone.

45
00:03:39,688 --> 00:03:44,719
diff=, merge= and filter= with names of your
own need a definition in configuration.

46
00:03:44,759 --> 00:03:46,108
That is the next video.

47
00:03:46,448 --> 00:03:53,523
Line endings. In one sentence: a path with
the text attribute is stored with LF line

48
00:03:53,563 --> 00:03:58,521
endings in the repository
whatever its endings are on disk.

49
00:03:58,561 --> 00:04:05,552
eol chooses the endings that a checkout
writes. And * text=auto lets Git decide per

50
00:04:05,592 --> 00:04:07,755
file which files are text.

51
00:04:08,095 --> 00:04:13,438
Read the third row again. With auto, a file
that is already stored with CRLF is left

52
00:04:13,478 --> 00:04:17,796
alone. So introducing text=auto
doesn't by itself rewrite a project.

53
00:04:18,136 --> 00:04:22,724
core.autocrlf is the older, per-user
mechanism. true converts in both directions,

54
00:04:22,764 --> 00:04:27,123
input only on the way in. It isn't
deprecated. It isn't a team policy either.

55
00:04:27,463 --> 00:04:32,080
The deliberate rewrite is 🟡
CAUTION: git add --renormalize ..

56
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:38,035
It applies the current conversion to
every tracked file and stages the result.

57
00:04:38,075 --> 00:04:42,005
The working tree, HEAD and
the branch are unchanged.

58
00:04:42,045 --> 00:04:45,975
New blobs are written where
the stored form changes.

59
00:04:46,015 --> 00:04:52,083
Preview with git status before committing,
and undo the staging with git restore

60
00:04:52,123 --> 00:04:52,922
--staged ..

61
00:04:53,262 --> 00:04:58,566
When to be careful. The renormalizing commit
touches every line of every converted

62
00:04:58,606 --> 00:05:03,974
file. Make it a commit of its own, announce
it, merge open branches first where you

63
00:05:04,014 --> 00:05:08,601
can, and list its ID in the file that
blame's ignore-revs option reads.

64
00:05:08,941 --> 00:05:13,981
Unmerged work will conflict on those
files. git merge -X renormalize, or

65
00:05:14,021 --> 00:05:19,908
merge.renormalize=true, normalizes all three
versions before merging and removes the

66
00:05:19,948 --> 00:05:25,694
conflicts that come from the conversion
alone. And mark files that must keep their

67
00:05:25,734 --> 00:05:31,550
bytes, vendor exports, fixtures with
deliberate CRLF, everything binary, with -text

68
00:05:31,590 --> 00:05:32,256
or binary.

69
00:05:34,096 --> 00:05:38,517
The textbook's analogy: labels on
folders in a shared filing cabinet.

70
00:05:38,557 --> 00:05:43,818
"Text". "Do not merge". "Show through viewer
X". Everyone sees the labels, because

71
00:05:43,858 --> 00:05:45,757
the labels are in the cabinet.

72
00:05:46,097 --> 00:05:48,238
Whether viewer X exists is a
matter of each reader's own desk.

73
00:05:48,278 --> 00:05:50,630
And here the analogy breaks: a
missing viewer produces no complaint.

74
00:05:50,970 --> 00:05:54,959
For line endings, the labels need no viewer.
text and eol are understood by every

75
00:05:54,999 --> 00:05:58,988
Git. That's why line-ending policy in
.gitattributes works for the contractor who

76
00:05:59,028 --> 00:06:00,380
never read the chat message.

77
00:06:02,220 --> 00:06:08,608
Try it now, on paper. A file is already
stored with CRLF, and you add * text=auto.

78
00:06:08,648 --> 00:06:15,036
Is the file rewritten? Pause me for thirty
seconds and write your answer, with the

79
00:06:15,076 --> 00:06:15,585
reason.

80
00:06:20,425 --> 00:06:28,254
Two files, two fates. sample.csv has an
explicit eol=crlf: stored with LF, checked

81
00:06:28,294 --> 00:06:30,653
out with CRLF everywhere.

82
00:06:30,993 --> 00:06:36,376
loader.py falls under text=auto: untouched
until the renormalization, then stored

83
00:06:36,416 --> 00:06:41,465
with LF. That was your answer, I hope:
not rewritten by the attribute alone.

84
00:06:41,505 --> 00:06:47,021
Watch the conversion happen on the way into
the index, while the file on disk keeps

85
00:06:47,061 --> 00:06:47,624
its CRLF.

86
00:06:47,964 --> 00:06:52,922
Only when Git writes the file again does
the working tree change, to the platform's

87
00:06:52,962 --> 00:06:54,186
form: LF on this Mac.

88
00:06:56,026 --> 00:07:01,047
Seven lines at the top: a default of
text=auto, shell scripts with LF, CSV files

89
00:07:01,087 --> 00:07:06,296
with CRLF, binaries, a merge driver for the
changelog, and two export-ignore lines.

90
00:07:06,336 --> 00:07:08,004
And a second file in data/.

91
00:07:08,344 --> 00:07:12,233
🟢 SAFE: git check-attr answers "which
attributes does Git see for this path".

92
00:07:12,273 --> 00:07:15,907
It is the first diagnostic for every
problem in this video and the next.

93
00:07:16,247 --> 00:07:22,200
Predict what data/encoder.bin shows. It
matches two lines: the star and *.bin.

94
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:24,275
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

95
00:07:29,115 --> 00:07:32,755
The later line won for text, and the
macro binary expanded into three unset

96
00:07:32,795 --> 00:07:36,337
attributes: diff, merge and text. With
-a you see only what is specified.

97
00:07:36,377 --> 00:07:39,380
Ask for named attributes to see
the unspecified state as well.

98
00:07:39,720 --> 00:07:45,241
Now precedence. The same pattern, one
file at the top and one below data/.

99
00:07:45,281 --> 00:07:51,178
Quick quiz for data/labels.csv, two
options. text is set, as the top file says.

100
00:07:51,218 --> 00:07:55,010
Or unset, as the nearer
file says. Say it out loud.

101
00:07:59,850 --> 00:08:05,324
Unset. For data/labels.csv the file in
data/ unset text, and eol still comes from

102
00:08:05,364 --> 00:08:09,749
the top-level file. Precedence is
decided attribute by attribute.

103
00:08:09,789 --> 00:08:14,174
Then .git/info/attributes, which
is not versioned, overrode both.

104
00:08:14,514 --> 00:08:17,124
One attribute that concerns
none of the conversions.

105
00:08:17,464 --> 00:08:21,217
docs and both attribute files are
tracked and absent from the archive.

106
00:08:21,257 --> 00:08:25,607
export-ignore is for what belongs in the
repository and not in a release tarball.

107
00:08:25,947 --> 00:08:31,014
loader.py and sample.csv are
i/crlf: CRLF in the repository.

108
00:08:31,054 --> 00:08:35,695
The attr/ column is empty.
First, the personal setting.

109
00:08:36,035 --> 00:08:42,158
Your setting normalized the file
you added: clean.py is i/lf.

110
00:08:42,198 --> 00:08:48,320
It didn't touch loader.py, which
is already stored with CRLF.

111
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:56,604
And the rest of the snippet shows a commit
made without the setting: split.py goes

112
00:08:56,644 --> 00:09:02,666
in with CRLF. That is the two
things core.autocrlf can't do.

113
00:09:03,006 --> 00:09:04,045
Now the attributes.

114
00:09:06,385 --> 00:09:10,765
You write three lines into .gitattributes.
You have edited no other file.

115
00:09:10,805 --> 00:09:12,764
Predict what git status -s shows.

116
00:09:13,104 --> 00:09:18,671
sample.csv is modified,
although you didn't touch it.

117
00:09:18,711 --> 00:09:27,028
It now has text set, so its stored CRLF
no longer matches what Git would store.

118
00:09:27,068 --> 00:09:35,491
loader.py and split.py fall under text=auto
and are left alone, because they are

119
00:09:35,531 --> 00:09:43,742
already stored with CRLF. After git add
--renormalize ., all three are staged.

120
00:09:44,082 --> 00:09:47,615
Every file is i/lf now, and
the attr/ column says why.

121
00:09:47,655 --> 00:09:53,172
The working tree column still shows CRLF
for several files: files on disk keep their

122
00:09:53,212 --> 00:09:55,554
endings until Git writes them again.

123
00:09:55,894 --> 00:10:02,429
git restore . here writes back files
that the script deleted a line earlier.

124
00:10:02,469 --> 00:10:09,609
In general it's 🔴 DANGEROUS, because it
overwrites uncommitted edits in the working

125
00:10:09,649 --> 00:10:15,058
tree. The rewritten loader.py and
split.py have LF on this Mac.

126
00:10:15,098 --> 00:10:20,076
sample.csv has CRLF on every
platform because of eol=crlf.

127
00:10:20,116 --> 00:10:24,747
And clean.py, which was not
rewritten, keeps its CRLF.

128
00:10:31,587 --> 00:10:33,235
Five mistakes to watch for.

129
00:10:33,575 --> 00:10:36,018
Making core.autocrlf the
team's line-ending policy.

130
00:10:36,058 --> 00:10:39,961
Root cause: it is configuration, which
covers what one person adds on one machine

131
00:10:40,001 --> 00:10:40,837
and is not cloned.

132
00:10:41,177 --> 00:10:46,202
Expecting * text=auto to fix files
that are already stored with CRLF.

133
00:10:46,242 --> 00:10:52,296
Root cause: with auto, Git leaves a file
alone when it is already stored with CRLF;

134
00:10:52,336 --> 00:10:55,012
the rewrite is git add --renormalize.

135
00:10:55,352 --> 00:11:00,433
Mixing the renormalization into a feature
commit. Root cause: it touches every line

136
00:11:00,473 --> 00:11:05,491
of every converted file, which hides the
feature and ruins blame unless the commit

137
00:11:05,531 --> 00:11:08,576
stands alone and is listed
for --ignore-revs-file.

138
00:11:08,916 --> 00:11:12,358
A directory pattern that seems not to
apply. Root cause: a pattern that matches a

139
00:11:12,398 --> 00:11:15,067
directory does not apply to the
files inside it; write docs/**.

140
00:11:15,407 --> 00:11:20,473
Guessing which rule applies to a path. Root
cause: precedence is per attribute and

141
00:11:20,513 --> 00:11:24,956
across several files; git check-attr
-a -- <path> reports what Git sees.

142
00:11:26,796 --> 00:11:36,451
Now, out of the lab. A data team with
engineers on macOS and Windows keeps Python

143
00:11:36,491 --> 00:11:44,112
sources, shell scripts and vendor
CSV exports in one repository.

144
00:11:44,152 --> 00:11:53,209
After the third pull request in which
every line of a file changed, the lead

145
00:11:53,249 --> 00:11:56,560
schedules the normalization.

146
00:11:56,900 --> 00:12:02,844
She merges the open branches she can. She
commits a .gitattributes with * text=auto,

147
00:12:02,884 --> 00:12:08,543
*.sh text eol=lf, and -text for the vendor
exports, which must keep their bytes.

148
00:12:08,583 --> 00:12:14,456
She runs git add --renormalize ., reads git
status before committing, and makes the

149
00:12:14,496 --> 00:12:17,448
result one commit with nothing else in it.

150
00:12:17,788 --> 00:12:21,424
The commit's ID goes into the
ignore-revs file that blame reads.

151
00:12:21,464 --> 00:12:25,846
The two branches that could not be merged
first are merged afterwards with -X

152
00:12:25,886 --> 00:12:30,269
renormalize, which removes the conflicts
that come from the conversion alone.

153
00:12:30,309 --> 00:12:35,093
The announcement to the team says what will
show as modified after the next pull and

154
00:12:35,133 --> 00:12:35,323
why.

155
00:12:37,163 --> 00:12:47,641
Your turn. Do Exercise 14.2, Level 1,
"Attributes that travel with the repository",

156
00:12:47,681 --> 00:12:53,597
in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

157
00:12:53,937 --> 00:12:59,230
Before every git check-attr, write the
state you expect for each attribute: set,

158
00:12:59,270 --> 00:13:03,164
unset, a value, or unspecified,
and the file it comes from.

159
00:13:03,204 --> 00:13:08,364
Before you add the attribute file, predict
which paths git status will show as

160
00:13:08,404 --> 00:13:10,764
modified without having been edited.

161
00:13:11,104 --> 00:13:15,396
The challenge is Exercise 14.5, Level
2, "A changelog that conflicts on every

162
00:13:15,436 --> 00:13:19,504
merge", in the same file. It needs the
next video for one of its answers.

163
00:13:21,344 --> 00:13:23,275
Answer out loud. I'll wait.

164
00:13:28,115 --> 00:13:30,635
A strong answer doesn't say
"everything shows as modified".

165
00:13:30,675 --> 00:13:34,150
It distinguishes what auto does with files
that are already stored with CRLF from

166
00:13:34,190 --> 00:13:36,928
what an explicit text does, and
predicts git status accordingly.

167
00:13:36,968 --> 00:13:40,443
It describes the renormalization by what
it changes: the index and new blobs, not

168
00:13:40,483 --> 00:13:41,181
the working tree.

169
00:13:41,521 --> 00:13:45,941
It then gives a rollout: a commit of its
own, the order relative to open branches,

170
00:13:45,981 --> 00:13:50,239
the merge option that removes conversion-only
conflicts, and the mechanism that

171
00:13:50,279 --> 00:13:54,427
keeps blame useful. It finishes with the
files that must be excluded and how.

172
00:13:56,267 --> 00:13:59,194
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

173
00:13:59,534 --> 00:14:03,874
.gitattributes is a tracked file that
attaches attributes to paths; git check-attr

174
00:14:03,914 --> 00:14:07,133
tells me which apply and precedence
is decided per attribute.

175
00:14:07,473 --> 00:14:11,147
The attribute travels with the repository;
a program it names is configuration and

176
00:14:11,187 --> 00:14:11,555
does not.

177
00:14:11,895 --> 00:14:18,383
text stores LF in the repository, eol
chooses the checkout form, and text=auto

178
00:14:18,423 --> 00:14:22,819
leaves files that are already
stored with CRLF alone.

179
00:14:23,159 --> 00:14:27,645
git add --renormalize . is the deliberate
rewrite, in one commit of its own.

180
00:14:27,985 --> 00:14:30,258
core.autocrlf is a per-user convenience
and cannot be a team policy.

181
00:14:32,098 --> 00:14:37,022
Read sections 14C.4 and
14C.5 of Chapter 14C.

182
00:14:37,362 --> 00:14:41,525
Today you gave a whole team one
line-ending policy with one tracked file.

183
00:14:41,565 --> 00:14:46,072
And the sentence from the start is yours
now: the attribute travels, the driver

184
00:14:46,112 --> 00:14:50,044
doesn't. Run git check-attr on a few
lab files before the next video.

185
00:14:50,084 --> 00:14:54,016
